edit('nsec-wireless','It is linear, so an attacker can flip bits in the ciphertext and fix up the checksum without knowing the key.','Its algebraic structure permits predictable checksum adjustments when bits are flipped; CRC-32 conventions include fixed initial/final terms, so the general relation is affine rather than simply CRC(a XOR b) = CRC(a) XOR CRC(b). It is not a keyed authenticator.','Align prose with correct quiz CRC affine formula and executed random checks.')
edit('nsec-wireless','The 6 GHz band (Wi-Fi 6E and 7) requires WPA3 or OWE, with no WPA2 fallback.','Wi-Fi certification requirements for 6 GHz call for WPA3 or Enhanced Open (OWE) with PMF, without WPA2 on that band. This does not prohibit a multi-band SSID from separately serving legacy clients on 2.4 or 5 GHz.','Scope requirement to certification/operating band; sameSSIDotherband legacypossible.',['https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/wireless/controller/9800/technical-reference/wpa3-dg.pdf'])
edit('nsec-wireless',"WPA2's four-way handshake derives a fresh PTK from the PMK and two nonces, with MICs proving both sides know the PMK.","WPA2's four-way handshake derives a PTK from the PMK, nonces and addresses; freshness depends on correct nonce generation and key installation, and MICs confirm possession of derived keying material.",'Summary omitted address inputs and assumed automatic freshness.')
edit('nsec-wireless',"WPA3's SAE prevents offline guessing and adds forward secrecy, with PMF mandatory; transition mode can be downgraded.","Correctly implemented pure SAE is designed to resist passive offline password verification and provide forward secrecy under its assumptions. Implementation flaws and WPA2 transition modes can weaken these properties.",'Align summary with actual side-channel/transition limitations.')
