edit('nsec-tls','In ordinary TLS only the server proves its identity; the client logs in later with a password or token. In **mutual TLS (mTLS)** the server sends a **CertificateRequest**, and the client answers with its own Certificate and CertificateVerify.','In a full server-certificate TLS handshake, the client need not supply a certificate and may authenticate at the application layer later. For certificate-based **mutual TLS (mTLS)**, the server requests a client certificate and the client supplies Certificate and CertificateVerify when authenticating. PSK resumption does not repeat these certificate messages.','Scope to certificate-based full handshakes; TLSsupportsPSKandanonymousclient appnotnecessarilylogs in.',rfc(8446))
